.TH SHARDPOST-SCRUB 1 "2026-10-03" "ShardPost 0.50.0" "ShardPost Manual"
.SH NAME
shardpost-scrub \- scrub accounts, submit the nightly batch scrub, sweep the pools for debris
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shardpost
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.B scrub
.br
.B shardpost
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.B scrub \-\-batch
.br
.B shardpost
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.B scrub \-\-sweep
.RB [ \-\-repair ]
.RB [ \-\-restart ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
These are commands of the server binary, not of
.BR shardpostctl (1):
they talk to the Ceph cluster directly, with the server's configuration
and Ceph credentials, and need neither a running server nor the admin
socket. They run in the scrub CronJob or in a Kubernetes Job of their
own, with the server's image.
.PP
Each of them registers in the admin store for as long as it runs
.RB ( servers/<host>\-<role>\-<random> ,
role
.B scrub
or, for the sweep,
.BR sweep ),
renews the registration every minute and records its orderly end; see
.BR shardpostctl-server (1).
Like every command of the server binary they check where the store's
own namespaces live before they start, and refuse a configuration that
does not match the cluster
.RI ( ceph.system_pool ,
see
.BR docs/configuration.md ).
.SS scrub
Scrubs accounts in this process and exits: the accounts of
.IR scrub.users ,
else every account of the account directory (once its backfill has
completed), else every namespace of a listing of the index pool. The
scrub checks and repairs what a crash can leave behind: reference counts,
index entries without blob, unfinished MOVEs and deliveries, quota
counters, and objects nothing points at. Where it looks for the last is
.IR scrub.orphan_source :
.B listing
lists each account's namespace in both pools,
.B shadow
(the default) does that and also works out what the intents name and
reports where the two differ,
.B intents
reads the intents only and lists nothing.
.B intents
is safe only when every server of the cluster runs 0.42.0 or later:
older servers write no removal intents, and a message they move or
expunge can then look like an orphan to the scrub. Nothing checks this
for you; switch only after the last older server is gone.
With
.I scrub.dry_run
it only reports.
.PP
The scrub also looks at the layout of the account's full-text index (see
.BR shardpostctl-fts (1)).
It reports, without repairing them, an object of the current layout 3
generation that is missing or carries another generation
.RB ( FTSShardsMissing ;
searches and deliveries of the account fail until
.B fts_rebuild
rebuilds the index) and an object of layout 2 in an account whose layout
2 objects should be gone
.RB ( FTSMixedLayout ,
written by a server before 0.45.0 after a wrong way back; repair with
.BR fts_rebuild ).
It enters an account with layout 3 in the presence index
.B ix.fts_layout
when it is missing there and removes an entry older than the grace
period whose account has no layout 3 any more
.RB ( FTSPresenceFixed ).
It reports a version marker of the index that does not fit its
generation
.RB ( FTSVersionDrift ,
.BR fts_version_drift ,
since 0.50.0): the marker
.B 3
of a dictionary without the identifier filter, which a version before
0.50.0 leaves when it converts a filtered generation (back to layout 2,
or a reshard that drops the filter from the layout record), is set to
.B 2
unless the run only reports (the dictionary is whole); a marker
.B 2
on a generation with the filter or the text preparation
.BR nosig ,
which a version before 0.50.0 writes when it gives such a generation
its first document ids while it is empty, is set to the marker of the
generation
.RB ( 3
or
.BR 4 ),
so that such a version no longer trusts it; a marker
.B 4
whose layout record lost its text preparation is only reported
.RB ( fts_layout
writes the record anew). It also keeps the
.B props
of the presence entry in step with the generations (counted in
.BR FTSPresenceFixed ).
The scrub job of the job system
.RB ( "shardpost scrub \-\-batch" ,
.BR "shardpostctl plan scrub" )
also removes an unfinished full-text conversion whose job does not run
any more or whose import reshard stopped sending its heartbeat.
.SS scrub \-\-batch
Plans the scrub of every account as one batch job with
.B skip_idle
and submits it; the servers' job runners carry it out. This is what the
nightly CronJob runs. See
.BR shardpostctl-job (1).
.SS scrub \-\-sweep
Finds what no account's data model names: debris that servers before
0.42.0 left without an intent, the objects of accounts purged before the
purge named its objects, full-text shards above a lowered
.IR server.fts_shards ,
layout 3 generations of the full-text index the layout record does not
name (a creation that lost a race, a conversion whose run crashed after
its record was removed). The data model names every generation of the
record (current, the conversion in progress, sources not removed yet),
which costs one read of the record per account.
It is a tool for a one-off cleanup, not a routine: it lists both pools
in every namespace, which costs a walk over every placement group of
each pool.
.PP
Pass 1 lists the index pool, then the data pool, and spills the object
names into spill objects in the namespace
.B ~accounts
of the index pool
.RI ( scrub.sweep_spill_shards
per pool). Pass 2 reads them one namespace at a time and compares the
names with the account's data model. What the model does not name is a
remnant. Without
.B \-\-repair
the sweep only reports; with it, a remnant older than
.I scrub.grace_seconds
is deleted (a blob through its reference count, so that a copy taken
meanwhile keeps it).
.PP
A namespace without registry is deleted only when the account directory
says the account is being purged
.RB ( purging ,
a purge that stopped after the registry went), or a purge recorded it
(the index
.B ix.purged
in
.BR ~accounts )
and the directory holds no entry for it; an active entry always wins. A
repairing sweep removes, when it ends, the records of the namespaces it
cleaned completely; registering the name again removes the record too.
Any other one is reported and left alone: a lost registry
.RB ( registry_missing ),
an account that only kept Sieve scripts before 0.42.0 or the leftovers
of a purge whose directory entry is gone
.RB ( unregistered ).
An account whose data model cannot be read in full (a registry record
that does not parse, an unreadable header, a registered mailbox without
its shard 0) and an account under an import lock are left alone. The
private \eSeen marks of a live public folder that were written before
0.42.0 are adopted (their registry key
.B ps:
is written) rather than deleted.
.PP
The sweep checkpoints in the cluster
.RB ( sweep/state
in
.BR ~accounts ):
run it again after it stopped and it goes on where it was; in pass 1 it
seeks to the listing position it reached, and lists a pool again when
the pool's
.B pg_num
changed meanwhile. A run that goes on works with the flags it is given
now: the namespaces it evaluated before keep what an earlier run without
.B \-\-repair
only reported (use
.B \-\-restart
to evaluate everything again). A sweep that ended has removed its spill
objects and state; the next one lists the pools anew. One sweep runs at a
time (a lock on
.B sweep/lock
in
.BR ~accounts ).
Lock, state and spill objects stay in the index pool when the rest of
.B ~accounts
lives in a system pool
.RI ( ceph.system_pool );
the account directory is read where it is. The store's own namespaces
and every other reserved
.B ~
namespace are never swept.
.PP
The report goes to standard output as one line of JSON after the log
lines: the run, the objects listed per pool, the namespaces looked at,
the remnants by class
.RB ( blob ,
.BR mailbox ,
.BR marks ,
.BR other ,
.B namespace
for objects of a namespace without registry,
.B fts
for kept full-text shards above the count), the namespaces whose account
was created again while the sweep looked at them
.RB ( recreated ;
nothing of them is deleted), how many were removed,
how many were left for being younger than the grace period, the marks
adopted, a few samples and the first errors.
.PP
Run the sweep with the servers' configuration (the same
.I server.fts_shards
and pools). A shard of the full-text index
.RB ( fts/ ,
.BR ftsh/ ,
.BR ftsd/ )
above the configured count in an account is reported as
.B fts
and never deleted: it may be a live one of servers configured with
more. Such shards are left by lowering
.IR server.fts_shards ;
to remove them on purpose, rebuild the account's index with
.B fts_rebuild
(see
.BR shardpostctl-job (1))
and delete the shards above the new count by hand
.RB ( "rados rm" ).
Any other remnant is deleted only at the object version that was judged,
so a write in between leaves it for the next run.
.PP
The sweep needs the cluster permission
.B mon 'allow r'
(it asks the monitors for each pool's
.BR pg_num );
the server's CephClient has it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
The server configuration (default: the environment variable
.BR SHARDPOST_CONFIG ).
.TP
.B \-\-batch
Submit the batch scrub instead of scrubbing in this process.
.TP
.B \-\-sweep
Sweep the pools (see above).
.TP
.B \-\-repair
With
.BR \-\-sweep :
delete the remnants older than
.IR scrub.grace_seconds .
Ignored with
.IR scrub.dry_run .
.TP
.B \-\-restart
With
.BR \-\-sweep :
drop a sweep that stopped (its spill objects included) and start over.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.TP
.I scrub.orphan_source
.BR listing ,
.B shadow
(default) or
.BR intents .
.TP
.I scrub.grace_seconds
Objects younger than this are left alone (3600); a scrub with
.B intents
and a sweep with
.B \-\-repair
leave at least the last ten minutes alone whatever it says.
.TP
.I scrub.intent_grace_seconds
How old an intent must be before the scrub takes it for the leftover of
a crash (3600).
.TP
.I scrub.sweep_spill_shards
Spill objects per pool of a new sweep (256).
.TP
.I scrub.sweep_clock_margin_seconds
Nothing written after the sweep read a namespace is a remnant; the line
lies this far before the read, on a whole second (60): object times
have whole seconds and come from the writing server's clock. It matters
only when a namespace takes the sweep longer than the grace period.
.TP
.I scrub.dry_run
Report only.
.SH EXAMPLES
What the sweep would remove, then remove it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpost scrub \-\-sweep | tail \-1 | jq '{listed, remnants, registry_missing}'
shardpost scrub \-\-sweep \-\-repair | tail \-1 | jq '{removed, young, errors}'
.RE
.fi
.PP
Start a sweep over after a long pause (the old spill objects go):
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpost scrub \-\-sweep \-\-restart
.RE
.fi
.PP
Scrub two accounts in this process, reporting only:
.PP
.nf
.RS
SCRUB_USERS=alice@example.org,bob@example.org SCRUB_DRY_RUN=true shardpost scrub
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The scrub, the submission or the sweep ended without an error (a sweep
may still have reported remnants or errors of single objects in its
report).
.TP
.B 1
Something failed: the configuration, the cluster, a scrub of an account,
another sweep holding the lock, or the sweep stopped (run it again to
continue).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR shardpostctl-job (1),
.BR shardpostctl-account (1)
